# Line endings, pinned. Added 2026-08-25, the day before the desktop's first pull.
#
# WHY. `core.autocrlf=true` was silently changing bytes under work in this repo three times in two
# nights — the dated case is exo_memory/loop/two_writers_registration_2026-08-25.md, where a pane's
# collision harness reported THREE CASES OF SILENT DATA LOSS that were all false: each clone got a
# CRLF working tree, the harness's re-insert anchor (a bare LF) stopped matching, and it deleted
# rows while failing to re-add them. The disappearance was attributed to git. Pinned off, all three
# inverted to CONFLICT. One contaminated harness invalidates everything it produced.
#
# MEASURED BEFORE WRITING THIS FILE, because the obvious fear is a repo-wide rewrite:
#   git add --renormalize .   ->  0 files.  The index is already LF throughout.
#   tracked text files 640    ->  0 carry CRLF in the working tree today.
# So this changes NOTHING on the machine that added it. What it changes is the NEXT checkout, here
# and anywhere else: without it, autocrlf converts all 640 on the way out, which is what a fresh
# clone gets. Every "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it" warning this repo
# prints is that conversion queued up and not yet spent.
#
# WHY eol=lf AND NOT text=auto ALONE. `text=auto` fixes what git STORES, which was never wrong here.
# The failure was in the WORKING TREE — what a tool opens and what an anchor matches. Only eol=lf
# makes that deterministic, and determinism across two machines is the whole point: this repo is
# about to have a second writer, and "which bytes are on disk" must not depend on which machine
# checked them out.
#
# Everything here is a Windows-native toolchain — node, PowerShell, Rust, Python — and all of them
# read LF without complaint. There is no CRLF consumer in this repo to protect.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, so it is not mistaken for more: it does not touch files already on disk,
# it does not settle whether the desktop's git config matches (it does not have to — that is the
# point of pinning it in-tree), and it does not fix the collision shapes in the registration above,
# which are a different subject that merely shared a symptom.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never line-ending-converted, never diffed as text. Listed explicitly rather than left to
# git's content heuristic, because a heuristic that guesses right today is not a decision.
*.png binary
*.bmp binary
*.ico binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary

# ── ADDED 2026-09-09, chair, lap D055 — from a corruption that had already happened ─────────────
# `* text=auto eol=lf` above converts anything git's heuristic calls TEXT. A raw terminal-capture
# fixture contains no NUL bytes, so the heuristic calls it text and strips its CRs ON THE WAY IN.
# Measured, not supposed: both .bin fixtures below were committed at e3a9b10 SHORT of the sizes
# their own author recorded from the laptop —
#   composer_empty_reads_busy      524,204 on disk vs 524,288 recorded (= 2^19, a capture cap)
#   composer_slash_command…empty   236,387 on disk vs 236,544 recorded
# and re-inserting a CR at every one of the 84 LFs in the first reproduces 524,288 EXACTLY, which
# is a prediction that could have failed. `git status` reports both CLEAN, because git compares the
# normalised working file to the blob — the third instrument this morning to report success over
# bytes that differ.
#
# TWO RULES, because they fail differently and neither covers the other:
*.bin binary
# and the extension-independent one — this directory holds raw screen captures and the NEXT capture
# format must not depend on anyone remembering to come back here. `.gitattributes` was written
# 2026-08-25; the first .bin in this repo was born 2026-09-09, which is exactly how the gap opened.
consonance/src-tauri/fixtures/screens/** binary

# ── ADDED 2026-09-27, pane B, lap L121 — both machines append to the same dated notes ─────────────
# The laptop and the desktop each committed on top of 5ced140 and both appended to the same files. Without
# a rule, the desktop's merge of origin stops on every such file with a conflict between two appends that
# are BOTH wanted. `merge=union` keeps both sides' lines, with no conflict markers.
#
# WHICH FILES, measured, not assumed. Every change after creation across first-parent history, from -U0 diffs:
# TAIL = only lines added after the old last line; MID = lines added elsewhere, none deleted; EDIT = any line
# deleted (handback/p-l121-merge-B_2026-09-27.md §1 has the command):
#   exo_memory/map/?.md               8 files   508 tail ·  6 mid · 6 edit
#   exo_memory/librarian/2026-*.md   33 files   607 tail ·  2 mid · 1 edit
#   exo_memory/third_place/2026-*.md 20 files    32 tail ·  0 mid · 0 edit
#
# LEFT OUT ON PURPOSE, because they are edited in place and union would silently keep BOTH versions of an
# edited line (shown in the throwaway repo, case N1):
#   exo_memory/librarian/LEDGER.md  (4 tail · 36 mid · 26 edit). This is why the rule is NOT librarian/*.md.
#   librarian/DOSSIER.md, README.md and the other undated notes · map/README.md · the frozen map/M-<date>.md
#   exo_memory/journal/  (dated files 59 tail · 11 mid · 5 edit; too many in-place corrections for union)
#   exo_memory/loop/, handback/, BOOT.md, SOURCE.md, every .json and .jsonl
#
# THE LIMIT THAT MATTERS: union never raises a conflict. If both machines EDIT the same line of one of these
# files, both lines stay and nothing says so. Six map edits and one librarian edit in the whole history say
# this is rare, not impossible. After a merge, read the diff of these files.
#
# AND THE ONE THAT BITES FIRST: git reads merge attributes from the checkout doing the merge. A desktop whose
# checkout predates this rule will still conflict when it merges a branch that carries it (case P2). Its
# runbook puts these lines in .git/info/attributes before the merge.
# The runbook: exo_memory/loop/runbook_D_merge_and_push_2026-09-27.md.
exo_memory/map/?.md merge=union
exo_memory/librarian/2026-*.md merge=union
exo_memory/third_place/2026-*.md merge=union
